notes.body:
And here's how flags with optional arguments, and flags with required arguments, are handled.  I tested this program:

```
#include <stdio.h>     /* for printf */
#include <stdlib.h>    /* for exit */
#include <getopt.h>

int
main(int argc, char **argv)
{
   int c;

   while (1) {
       int option_index = 0;
       static struct option long_options[] = {
           {"optional",  optional_argument, 0,  'o' },
           {"required",  required_argument, 0,  'r' },
           {0,           0,                 0,  0 }
       };

       c = getopt_long(argc, argv, "o::r:",
                long_options, &option_index);
       if (c == -1)
           break;

       switch (c) {
       case 'o':
           printf("option %s", long_options[option_index].name);
           if (optarg)
               printf(" with arg %s", optarg);
           printf("\n");
           break;
       case 'r':
           printf("option %s", long_options[option_index].name);
           if (optarg)
               printf(" with arg %s", optarg);
           printf("\n");
           break;
       case '?':
           break;

       default:
           printf("?? getopt returned character code 0%o ??\n", c);
       }
   }

   if (optind < argc) {
       printf("non-option ARGV-elements: ");
       while (optind < argc)
           printf("%s ", argv[optind++]);
       printf("\n");
   }

   exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

on macOS 10.15.4 (with a BSD `getopt_long()`; I'm guessing all the BSD `getopt_long()` implementations behave similarly, although that may not be the case), Ubuntu 20.04 (with a GNU libc `getopt_long()`), and Solaris 11 (with Sun's/Oracle's own `getopt_long()`?).

They also implement an extension not documented in the Single UNIX Specification, but implemented by all three versions of `getopt_long()` - in the single-character option string, if an option character is followed by *two* colons, the single-character option takes an optional argument.  Given that we're using `getopt_long()`, which isn't in the Single UNIX Specification, and are using a GNU libc version of `getopt_long()` on systems that don't have it, the Single UNIX Specification's lack of that feature probably wouldn't matter to us.

So the behavior is:

```
$ ./opttest -o
option optional
$ ./opttest -o frob
option optional
non-option ARGV-elements: frob 
$ ./opttest -ofrob
option optional with arg frob
$ ./opttest --optional
option optional
$ ./opttest --optional frob
option optional
non-option ARGV-elements: frob 
$ ./opttest --optional=frob
option optional with arg frob
$ ./opttest -r
opttest: option requires an argument -- r
$ ./opttest -r frob
option optional with arg frob
$ ./opttest -rfrob
option optional with arg frob
$ ./opttest --required
opttest: option `--required' requires an argument
$ ./opttest --required frob
option required with arg frob
$ ./opttest --required=frob
option required with arg frob
```

So, for a flag that takes an optional argument, you *can't* separate the flag from its argument with a space - for a single-letter option, you must immediately follow the flag with the argument (`-xnoascii`) and, for a long option, you must separate the flag and the argument with just an = sign (`--hexdump=noascii`).

I.e., `-x noascii` won't work; that'll be interpreted as `-x`, with no argument, followed by the first non-flag argument, "noascii".

@johnthacker Thanks for pointing out those two oversights.

Regarding your earlier comment about not being able to use `-X <hexoption>`, that would have been my preference too.

I did seriously considered renaming this new long option to `--hex <hexoption>`. (And there would be no objection from me if someone decided to do so in a followup.)

